#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "37f0d2c0-c174-5478-a007-5fb3d32bafe2" :question/math-academy-id "e-16569" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2] \\times [0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](images/0f/0f5dda7937b1ffed646730040efe11464d46ef1f3ec6ae5dacf84075bd5be01b.png)\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T.$" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/c3/c3ec90119193ca9c0cb886bc7b7c698eb155e6a801fd267c0a17a0d329eff84d.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/ef/eff9e5b8ab46002bd82fa42d59f60b1bb78ca04228d7c73b52d131d222ccb894.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}y-x-2 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(2)-1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}(2)-1-2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2}-2+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "1fa3ed18-5fb9-53b2-a067-5db623a289d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16570" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3] \\times [-2,0] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](images/89/891b79a977ea7d9f4062d3614d7001ade71cfc10de54fcbae6608eebed7e8d1a.png)\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 0 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf{x}_1 = \\big[ 3, \\: -1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/95/95e234c5c9b32e1ea965f1cd184563699fbd2793d51a9d1e4ec96ffb4e42dc50.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+y-4 \\\\ x+{y}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(3) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(-1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{2}+(-1)-4 \\\\ 3+(-1)^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{30}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{13}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}2.308 \\\\ -0.846\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203827 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "5da362d4-cb58-5b51-acc0-582e3a0d42e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-16571" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](images/c3/c3c2b2e7a9d5b2b8f76499b0ad62d628f9d79bc7c41b6bcaf9926df4f368cdbf.png)\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\nJ_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](images/13/13f9eb9850462317be5a5c7da22fe53ee89b24d9ce1a9226c2d0ee1e6355ba19.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/8b/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2(1) & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}-(1)^{2} \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](images/d5/d5eb5a91fdd96bf6ca50539d2119a97ce9e5600b3114f708cda4d95a047e8b81.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a8100ac-e347-5b38-b6c6-bb7a2b8c0727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a8100ac-e347-5b38-b6c6-bb7a2b8c0727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291303} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c460b7-9d26-5f20-b5ee-9a0fc390ea0e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf791c87-709f-5428-b4b4-6d3c687972f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.833" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf791c87-709f-5428-b4b4-6d3c687972f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.833" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291513} :answer-field/id #uuid "57f256ed-ce3a-5b75-9072-5fb4222f2497" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c393a2-8fbe-5fde-bcd8-175c9a07323b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c393a2-8fbe-5fde-bcd8-175c9a07323b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290871} :answer-field/id #uuid "73c83487-cc97-5bc0-935d-7dd74e2026dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3df2f3-7adb-5a46-a733-e00d9b6d9f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db3df2f3-7adb-5a46-a733-e00d9b6d9f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291548} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5d77e1-ce57-5fc9-af23-cf7376ef8646" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76b77696-fb71-5bf3-bbfc-fa9d52e3daf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-255856" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-3}} | $3$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.833$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) & 2(1) \\\\ 3(1)^{2} & 3(1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-2 \\\\ (1)^{3}+(1)^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.833 \\\\ 0.833\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5d2d6e-b12c-5626-b69d-57c8c55c1360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.545" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5d2d6e-b12c-5626-b69d-57c8c55c1360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.545" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566453} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e64f3c-51b1-5434-ba7f-f814d56f0237" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2d3b902-846f-5938-a220-235340dd6162" :question/math-academy-id "q-256226" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}\\,≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1.091$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+x-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(2)+1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}(2)+(1)-3 \\\\ (1)(2)^{2}-(2)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{11} \\\\ \\frac{17}{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.091 \\\\ 1.545\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a313b0c4-c737-5636-9bcb-ba4b207da3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a313b0c4-c737-5636-9bcb-ba4b207da3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566680} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1f2da94-6ab9-57e0-8451-0147b8fd3aab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0b64b0d-c018-5cb8-8dc3-4328291139b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-256527" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-0.769$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3(-1)^{2} & -2(1) \\\\ -2(-1) & 3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ {1}^{3}-(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{13} \\\\ \\frac{11}{13}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.769 \\\\ 0.846\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100c6fc7-a891-5c69-803a-28cb5b8b515e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "100c6fc7-a891-5c69-803a-28cb5b8b515e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566395} :answer-field/id #uuid "3203cf69-7295-5e3e-bbc0-aa6c095cde61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7348f6b-50f4-5829-823f-60cfb1d3d3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-256530" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-1) & -3(1)^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}-(1)^{3}-1 \\\\ (1)^{2}-2(-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15771d00-08c8-5812-86dd-33e1aaa96bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.533" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15771d00-08c8-5812-86dd-33e1aaa96bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.533" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566404} :answer-field/id #uuid "931a26e8-842b-5c6f-984b-87c064fbdfc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f2f93f2-7657-5014-9280-e3a013d49cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-256532" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [-3,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-1.967$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-3x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-2) & 2(-2) \\\\ -3 & 3(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}-10 \\\\ (-2)^{3}-3(-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 12-(-4)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{38}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{59}{30}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.533 \\\\ -1.967\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbc551c-181f-5f3e-a9ff-6045699e2f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbc551c-181f-5f3e-a9ff-6045699e2f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566643} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f48a5d-fa33-5262-b670-209385cf37a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58eb000c-0023-5bbe-878d-2a7d597e6bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58eb000c-0023-5bbe-878d-2a7d597e6bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566531} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e34f53-7ad4-586d-ba06-4f7d04fa20c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a68de8d-2409-54b8-80ec-b559058f032c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a68de8d-2409-54b8-80ec-b559058f032c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566534} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1184f4-0e80-5426-ac23-1a3f96154ed6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54fa8a66-cee0-5c97-b60e-5706160e8699" :question/math-academy-id "q-256537" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0.5,1]\\times [1,1.5]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$].$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$3$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6(1) & 2(1) \\\\ {1}^{3} & 3(1)(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4 \\\\ (1)(1)^{3}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b24268-03af-5f61-b769-ce6b7a31eef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.803" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00b24268-03af-5f61-b769-ce6b7a31eef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.803" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566361} :answer-field/id #uuid "9191cff1-2747-5267-a434-7d58f847ae4a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566944} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af15c4f2-249a-53da-8dd6-bdf7aa55a708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af15c4f2-249a-53da-8dd6-bdf7aa55a708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566711} :answer-field/id #uuid "d536fe21-51ff-527b-9668-71612e6ac541" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df6b1269-b576-51fc-8968-9319e6cd6df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-256550" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [-3,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-1}}\n        </td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-2}}\n        </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(-2)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(-2)(-2)-2 & (-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{4}-(-2)^{2}+3 \\\\ (-2)^{2}(-2)-2(-2)+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-32)\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{152}[\\begin{aligned}56 \\\\ -122\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.632 \\\\ -2.803\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4b5ce92-2b6a-5730-b71e-96b42f420f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5ce92-2b6a-5730-b71e-96b42f420f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566754} :answer-field/id #uuid "e22a6679-528f-5264-bb4c-902dfac6798f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb31b79d-a056-5d3d-8cde-aa8541962fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb31b79d-a056-5d3d-8cde-aa8541962fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566735} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c2df3c-7eaa-56b9-b6cf-bcb3519b90d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1b28b00-c68b-528a-b185-95ca8f4c67f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1b28b00-c68b-528a-b185-95ca8f4c67f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566819} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d0ae9a-4a26-5d5a-a08c-a9c0e3dad3d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd1e334c-d1a6-5f52-b1e4-f36e41200183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd1e334c-d1a6-5f52-b1e4-f36e41200183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566768} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabddd47-4f3d-5d1f-aa0d-9de39e3fb1d3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc018c2-2bf7-5faf-a17d-6c2e774a7e12" :question/math-academy-id "q-256552" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [-3,-1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,-2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$4$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$2$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-4\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$2.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-y+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-y+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(1)(-2) & {1}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-(-2)^{2}+1 \\\\ (1)^{2}(-2)-(-2)+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ -2.75\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127832c5-bcac-5c2d-871c-4ae610d015cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "127832c5-bcac-5c2d-871c-4ae610d015cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566399} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5af280c-c756-592a-9a39-676c0b8ff81b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc32bcd-337a-5152-8c70-afda51f6c38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc32bcd-337a-5152-8c70-afda51f6c38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566514} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f26550d-f8be-5b43-b6c1-b97ec83cf43b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5800980a-2f76-57ac-b91f-63be30605046" :question/math-academy-id "q-256559" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ {x}^{3}y-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-1}}\n        </td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-2}}\n        </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ {x}^{3}y-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ {x}^{3}y-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & -4(1)^{3} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}(1) & (1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-(1)^{4}+1 \\\\ (1)^{3}(1)-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-(-4)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e728b7-c815-51fd-9f4c-dd105d585f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07e728b7-c815-51fd-9f4c-dd105d585f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566380} :answer-field/id #uuid "06236914-13d5-53d3-9565-9058e6c61417" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e67dad84-1c40-5799-a25b-dbde5aaa3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.643" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e67dad84-1c40-5799-a25b-dbde5aaa3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.643" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566830} :answer-field/id #uuid "06b2d87b-0e82-536c-817c-50a66418bf1b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e45889b-7016-5529-8bcf-104005e4ff74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e45889b-7016-5529-8bcf-104005e4ff74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566569} :answer-field/id #uuid "98014e85-3eba-5e10-947e-4413f9a7d682" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b02aa3f2-6bd0-541b-a15e-4df7d8918483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b02aa3f2-6bd0-541b-a15e-4df7d8918483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566714} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e20e82-8da3-5240-8cef-338aa19c81ab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "895909c1-3f1c-55f2-a7ef-724fd328cb42" :question/math-academy-id "q-256581" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-1]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[-1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$5$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-8$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$0.786$\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}-y+10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{5}-y+10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5(-1)^{4} & -1 \\\\ 1 & (-1)-2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{5}-(1)+10 \\\\ (-1)(1)-{1}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{37}{14} \\\\ \\frac{11}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.643 \\\\ 0.786\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37193a9b-3b0b-5473-8328-e36aa8fce39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37193a9b-3b0b-5473-8328-e36aa8fce39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566475} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ffe2424-7cf6-5e89-b5ad-f705f598ccf4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7a10793-7256-5e28-8063-441fe3609297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7a10793-7256-5e28-8063-441fe3609297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566832} :answer-field/id #uuid "d871611a-f9ad-5f63-8bde-09c676ffe125" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566975} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9fb16e-5d6c-5bc3-982a-69cc2e91be40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f9fb16e-5d6c-5bc3-982a-69cc2e91be40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566458} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd7efa9c-dece-5382-95eb-5f0e9511093a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f8edea-8dce-534e-8c1b-b923aa29a024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44f8edea-8dce-534e-8c1b-b923aa29a024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566498} :answer-field/id #uuid "f11392da-3e72-5382-8128-a02b6e0cbaeb" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a4b8cfe-b1ff-5fee-9d79-bf022b1f4764" :question/math-academy-id "q-256585" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-1]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[-1,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$8$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-2.095$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y+7) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}-y+7) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & 3(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}-2 & -(-1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-1)(2)^{3}-2+7 \\\\ (-1)^{3}-(-1)(2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8 & -13 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{44}{21} \\\\ \\frac{23}{21}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.095 \\\\ 1.095\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f5647f-97ad-59f0-ab92-f833fed63b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.308" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06f5647f-97ad-59f0-ab92-f833fed63b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.308" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566375} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da0d6c5-6eb3-52c0-aa61-50aa3fb27219" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "977b78e7-1471-5b4e-b2ae-73fdfb213d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-256606" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1.497$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{3}+2(1)(2) & -3(2)^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}(2)-4 & {1}^{3}+4(2)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-{2}^{3}+(1)^{2}(2)-3 \\\\ (1)^{3}(2)+{2}^{4}-4(1)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 33\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{8\\cdot 33-(-11)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}33 & 11 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{286}[\\begin{aligned}-88 \\\\ 144\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.308 \\\\ 1.497\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc9d4c96-05e4-5a83-91bf-325ca98903d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9d4c96-05e4-5a83-91bf-325ca98903d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566737} :answer-field/id #uuid "706c5572-8644-5d0d-8248-7cf8eb39ab6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f62c3fa-de25-562d-8e5e-e66a3ff052f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f62c3fa-de25-562d-8e5e-e66a3ff052f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566573} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b540632-ba71-5357-a108-ba013264f885" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "085133b9-a034-5d48-855d-a7e2bd43acae" :question/math-academy-id "q-256700" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,-4]\\times [1,4]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,4]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-2.5,\\,2.5]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(-2.5) & -2(2.5) \\\\ 2(-2.5) & 2(2.5)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(-2.5)^{2}-(2.5)^{2}-4 \\\\ (-2.5)^{2}+(2.5)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-10)\\cdot 5-(-5)\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{75}[\\begin{aligned}33.75 \\\\ -33.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.05 \\\\ 2.05\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b78f08e1-884c-5722-b84d-d4f409a242b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.087" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b78f08e1-884c-5722-b84d-d4f409a242b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.087" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566728} :answer-field/id #uuid "71613501-3952-5c5a-ae70-a4098a087188" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3a164c2-857d-5c9c-bc3a-8f829b1fdd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.474" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a164c2-857d-5c9c-bc3a-8f829b1fdd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.474" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566681} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b63372-18c6-5b5d-b99f-ed271dd103b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f06ea59f-1a95-56b5-bc69-f41150e63482" :question/math-academy-id "q-256714" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1.5,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-1.5) & -2(1) \\\\ 3(-1.5)^{2}-3(1)^{2} & -6(-1.5)(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1.5)^{2}-(1)^{2}-1 \\\\ (-1.5)^{3}-3(-1.5)(1)^{2}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 3.75 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ -0.875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 9-(-2)\\cdot 3.75}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ -3.75 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ -0.875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{19.5}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1.6875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.474 \\\\ 1.087\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a185eb64-e91b-531a-a4a0-f01d400f68b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.426" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a185eb64-e91b-531a-a4a0-f01d400f68b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.426" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566677} :answer-field/id #uuid "938c2603-3376-599e-982c-2652836c0f65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad4ed577-bba0-502f-92eb-a64a5e86d766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.298" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad4ed577-bba0-502f-92eb-a64a5e86d766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.298" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566708} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5647fd8-cf3b-56aa-bf50-9f000a2e82a8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2448b07-2d2b-5e97-89d6-b8d2e48ba3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-256722" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [-2,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)-(-2) & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}-(1)(-2)-4 \\\\ 1-(-2)^{3}-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-12)-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{47}[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{67}{47} \\\\ -\\frac{61}{47}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.426 \\\\ -1.298\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}]}
